{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UrfVFXBYF9aAffmeALCzmd","type":"tool_result","content":"2571\t### 10.8 Amendments after phase 7 (2026-10-14)\n2572\t\n2573\t**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: inventory the row custody, recovery and acceptance amendments against code.\n2574\t\n2575\t| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n2576\t|---|---|---|---|\n2577\t| P1 | §2.5-2.6; tuples §5.5 | fork nil in every row; exclusive custody by kind, only five keys | owner fork ruling and `custody-baseline` |\n2578\t| P2 | §7.1 | header/enrollment checks follow walk/frame validation; retained serves survive retry | `lift-task` order, 7a D4 |\n2579\t| P3 | §7.2; tuples §5.4, §8-9 | three per-tree stages, custody once, then attach; grant uses pack address; install derivation checks | 7a/7b code and zero-attach law |\n2580\t| P4 | §7.2; UCF §7.7.4 | fenced lower; recovery format 2 with one pack and local metadata | no second snapshot, no runnable recovery |\n2581\t| P5 | tuples §7; §11 | sorted emission, pack/root identity split, same-root reordered equal variant | root-inclusive baseline; 7a D2 |\n2582\t| P6 | §8.4; UCF §7.11.1 | entry conditions met; Stage D/E port and remaining host gates distinguished | 7d report is JVM evidence only |\n2583\t| P7 | §11 | 6b Q3 closed; Q4 op-ids closed, correlation left open | implemented checks, no invented validation |\n2584\t| P8 | §4.6, §5.3; UCF §7.11 | canonical kernel and retained backends, documented fragment | owner ruling: kept after phase 8, not widened |\n2585\t\n2586\t## 11. Owner questions\n2587\t\n2588\tRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\n2589\tand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\n2590\tlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\n2591\tEach is stated as the code behaves today; phase-7 closures below leave\n2592\tall other questions open.\n2593\t\n2594\t**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7a D2 corrects the plan's equal-baseline variant claim.\n2595\tThe baseline includes `:yin.k/root`. Re-emitting an id-counter change\n2596\tchanges that root and is `:variant-conflict`. The equal-baseline encoding\n2597\tvariant retains the same root with another row order. `emit` sorts rows;\n2598\t`decode` still accepts unsorted packs, so one root/one pack is an emission\n2599\tproperty, not a reader restriction.\n2600\t\n2601\t- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n2602\t  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n2603\t  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n2604\t  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n2605\t  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n2606\t  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n2607\t  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n2608\t  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n2609\t  to it?\n2610\t- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n2611\t  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n2612\t  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n2613\t  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n2614\t  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n2615\t  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n2616\t  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n2617\t- **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7b implements the owner-required install checks before attach.\n2618\t  **6b Q3, CLOSED as implemented: install response verification.**\n2619\t  `check-install!` verifies phase, response status, manifest module/export\n2620\t  shape, image admission and blocked child, then requires a non-nil parent\n2621\t  equal to response `:yin.link/id`. The manifest's semantic derivation\n2622\t  address must hash the response's derivation record; its input equals\n2623\t  the manifest tree and its output equals the image vector's A.\n2624\t  This verifies response self-consistency; binding to composition-known\n2625\t  content addresses remains the linker's fetch/admission responsibility.\n2626\t- **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7a implements the op-id grammar; correlation checks remain narrower.\n2627\t  **6b Q4, CLOSED for operation ids; correlation agreement remains open.**\n2628\t  `custody-baseline` checks retained put/FFI-request/link-request ids\n2629\t  throughout the tree: exact sequences below next-op-seq, uniqueness,\n2630\t  origin required, and no id naming the root's own occurrence. Fork trees\n2631\t  refuse any id. Lower checks protection/enrollment in both directions\n2632\t  before attachment. The broader cursor-cell stream, envelope-id and module\n2633\t  correlation agreement promised by draft §8 is not implemented as a\n2634\t  general check; Q3's install-parent agreement is implemented.\n2635\t- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n2636\t  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n2637\t  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n2638\t  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n2639\t  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n2640\t  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n2641\t  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n2642\t  before attachment?\n2643\t- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n2644\t  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n2645\t  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n2646\t  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n2647\t  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n2648\t  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n2649\t  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n2650\t  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n2651\t  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n2652\t  constant-dead code?\n2653\t- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n2654\t  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n2655\t  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n2656\t  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n2657\t  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n2658\t- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n2659\t  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n2660\t  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n2661\t  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n2662\t  entry point?\n2663\t- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n2664\t  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n2665\t  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n2666\t  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n2667\t  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n2668\t- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n2669\t  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n2670\t  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n2671\t\n2672\t**Revision 5 (after 6c) adds the three questions the rulings left\n2673\t(`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`); they are\n2674\tnumbered on their own, as 6c Q1-Q3, so the 6b numbering above stands.**\n2675\t\n2676\t- **6c Q1, the native-lane matrix scope (changes acceptance counts).\n2677\t  ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13.** The de Bruijn lanes run the\n2678\t  shared fragment: law 4 on 61 of the 89 cases, law 5 on 350 of the 534\n2679\t  transfers, tail acceptance on rows 6 and 20 only.\n2680\t  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner retains the backends after phase 8 on the same fragment.\n2681\t  The canonical lane keeps the full matrix; the retained backends keep\n2682\t  this fragment (§4.6).\n2683\t  The owner confirmed the matrix of 61 law-4 cases and 350 law-5\n2684\t  transfers as normative; §5.3 and §8.3 items 10-11 state it so.\n2685\t- **6c Q2, the retained-FFI seam (kernel edit, own slice). Open.** Land\n2686\t  6c-post-D2, the four-line response-pair seam in both de Bruijn kernels\n2687\t  (§4.3; half a day), raising law 5 to 366 and 96 and emptying\n2688\t  `unlowerable`.\n2689\t  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: kernel retention does not widen the fragment.\n2690\t  The question remains open; the phase-7 ruling retains the current\n2691\t  fragment, so these extra transfers are not acceptance claims.\n2692\t- **6c Q3, closures in the free environment (information; a decision\n2693\t  only if the owner disagrees). Open.** Closures and continuations in\n2694\t  the task-wide free environment are refused on the native lanes (§6.3,\n2695\t  R3) and admitted everywhere else. If any real composition seeds a de\n2696\t  Bruijn kernel's free environment with a closure, say so and R3\n2697\t  becomes a design question instead of a refusal.\n2698\t\n2699\t---\n2700\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14576d0d-3405-4311-bbba-ad13d850656b","uuid":"e131ccbd-15af-42cd-933d-532c0ca57e22","timestamp":"2026-10-14T00:03:30.234Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p8plan/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md","content":"### 10.8 Amendments after phase 7 (2026-10-14)\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: inventory the row custody, recovery and acceptance amendments against code.\n\n| # | Clause | What changed | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P1 | §2.5-2.6; tuples §5.5 | fork nil in every row; exclusive custody by kind, only five keys | owner fork ruling and `custody-baseline` |\n| P2 | §7.1 | header/enrollment checks follow walk/frame validation; retained serves survive retry | `lift-task` order, 7a D4 |\n| P3 | §7.2; tuples §5.4, §8-9 | three per-tree stages, custody once, then attach; grant uses pack address; install derivation checks | 7a/7b code and zero-attach law |\n| P4 | §7.2; UCF §7.7.4 | fenced lower; recovery format 2 with one pack and local metadata | no second snapshot, no runnable recovery |\n| P5 | tuples §7; §11 | sorted emission, pack/root identity split, same-root reordered equal variant | root-inclusive baseline; 7a D2 |\n| P6 | §8.4; UCF §7.11.1 | entry conditions met; Stage D/E port and remaining host gates distinguished | 7d report is JVM evidence only |\n| P7 | §11 | 6b Q3 closed; Q4 op-ids closed, correlation left open | implemented checks, no invented validation |\n| P8 | §4.6, §5.3; UCF §7.11 | canonical kernel and retained backends, documented fragment | owner ruling: kept after phase 8, not widened |\n\n## 11. Owner questions\n\nRevisions 2-3 left none: the reviewer accepted the engine-less profile\nand rejected the merged-`E` free environment. **Revision 4 (after 6b)\nlists the design choices the merged code made that no ruling covers.**\nEach is stated as the code behaves today; phase-7 closures below leave\nall other questions open.\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7a D2 corrects the plan's equal-baseline variant claim.\nThe baseline includes `:yin.k/root`. Re-emitting an id-counter change\nchanges that root and is `:variant-conflict`. The equal-baseline encoding\nvariant retains the same root with another row order. `emit` sorts rows;\n`decode` still accepts unsorted packs, so one root/one pack is an emission\nproperty, not a reader restriction.\n\n- **Q1, install children, the parked-child scenario.** The engine never\n  retains an install child at a quiescent safepoint except `:blocked` at\n  phase `:parked` (§5.2 B1). The carrier still admits phase `:running`\n  (never engine-reachable at a safepoint) and the lower refuses any\n  child kind but `:blocked`. Should the row table and both checks\n  narrow to the one reachable state (phase `:parked`, child `:blocked`),\n  or does the owner want a reachable parked-child install scenario,\n  which is an engine change (`advance-install`), with row 11 rewritten\n  to it?\n- **Q2, census agreement on lower.** The draft §8 step 9 requires the\n  carried `requires`/`code` to agree **exactly** with a census recomputed\n  from the rows (a declared segment not reached, or a reached segment\n  not declared, is `:yin.k/undecodable`). `check-dependencies!` checks\n  only that `:yin.k/segments` equals the key set of `code`; a vector\n  carried but referenced by no row is accepted and attached. Accept the\n  narrower check, or require the recomputed census?\n- **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7b implements the owner-required install checks before attach.\n  **6b Q3, CLOSED as implemented: install response verification.**\n  `check-install!` verifies phase, response status, manifest module/export\n  shape, image admission and blocked child, then requires a non-nil parent\n  equal to response `:yin.link/id`. The manifest's semantic derivation\n  address must hash the response's derivation record; its input equals\n  the manifest tree and its output equals the image vector's A.\n  This verifies response self-consistency; binding to composition-known\n  content addresses remains the linker's fetch/admission responsibility.\n- **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7a implements the op-id grammar; correlation checks remain narrower.\n  **6b Q4, CLOSED for operation ids; correlation agreement remains open.**\n  `custody-baseline` checks retained put/FFI-request/link-request ids\n  throughout the tree: exact sequences below next-op-seq, uniqueness,\n  origin required, and no id naming the root's own occurrence. Fork trees\n  refuse any id. Lower checks protection/enrollment in both directions\n  before attachment. The broader cursor-cell stream, envelope-id and module\n  correlation agreement promised by draft §8 is not implemented as a\n  general check; Q3's install-parent agreement is implemented.\n- **Q5, no re-discharge on lower.** Free names are discharged at lift\n  only (§7.1); the lower checks carried segments, trees, store census,\n  closures and primitives, and a name a closure resolves through the\n  registry is a composition prerequisite (§2.5). A receiver lacking that\n  registry entry accepts the carrier and fails at run time\n  (`Unable to resolve symbol`). Accept, or require the lower to\n  re-discharge registry-answered names against the receiver's registry\n  before attachment?\n- **Q6, conservatism of the live-code rule.** `live-pcs` follows both\n  successor edges of every branch and enters every reachable closure\n  body, with no constant propagation. The harness's `(if false (fn []\n  ghost) (next-of c))` case transports only because the lift happens at\n  a safepoint **after** the branch (the resume pc is past it, so the\n  dead arm is unreachable by edges); the same program lifted at a\n  safepoint **before** the branch would refuse `ghost` although the arm\n  can never run. Is \"reachable by control-flow edges, never by value\"\n  the intended precision, accepting the false refusals it implies for\n  constant-dead code?\n- **Q7, host-class primitives behind a shadow.** `:host-state-primitive`\n  refuses only when the name reaches the primitive step of the answering\n  order; the same name bound in `E` or the active store lifts (codex\n  round 2 confirmed). Intended, or should a host-class primitive name\n  refuse regardless of shadowing?\n- **Q8, preflight attachment cost.** `attaches?` validates and analyses\n  every carried vector (and every install image) by attaching it into a\n  throwaway receiver, and restore attaches it again. Codex found it pure\n  and only duplicated CPU. Accept, or ask the kernel for a validate-only\n  entry point?\n- **Q9, Law 2 outside a fresh receiver.** The id counter is adopted as\n  `max(local, carried)`; a receiver whose construction mints more ids\n  than the carrier relifts to a different root (draft §9 already scopes\n  the law to the fresh-receiver context). No harness case has a\n  non-fresh receiver. Add one as a documented non-law, or leave it?\n- **Q10, the `:running` wire phase's future.** If Q1 narrows the phase\n  set, row 11 disappears from the matrix and the `:install` row's phase\n  slot becomes a constant; if not, row 11 stays a synthetic case. Which?\n\n**Revision 5 (after 6c) adds the three questions the rulings left\n(`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md`); they are\nnumbered on their own, as 6c Q1-Q3, so the 6b numbering above stands.**\n\n- **6c Q1, the native-lane matrix scope (changes acceptance counts).\n  ACCEPTED by the owner on 2026-10-13.** The de Bruijn lanes run the\n  shared fragment: law 4 on 61 of the 89 cases, law 5 on 350 of the 534\n  transfers, tail acceptance on rows 6 and 20 only.\n  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner retains the backends after phase 8 on the same fragment.\n  The canonical lane keeps the full matrix; the retained backends keep\n  this fragment (§4.6).\n  The owner confirmed the matrix of 61 law-4 cases and 350 law-5\n  transfers as normative; §5.3 and §8.3 items 10-11 state it so.\n- **6c Q2, the retained-FFI seam (kernel edit, own slice). Open.** Land\n  6c-post-D2, the four-line response-pair seam in both de Bruijn kernels\n  (§4.3; half a day), raising law 5 to 366 and 96 and emptying\n  `unlowerable`.\n  **Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: kernel retention does not widen the fragment.\n  The question remains open; the phase-7 ruling retains the current\n  fragment, so these extra transfers are not acceptance claims.\n- **6c Q3, closures in the free environment (information; a decision\n  only if the owner disagrees). Open.** Closures and continuations in\n  the task-wide free environment are refused on the native lanes (§6.3,\n  R3) and admitted everywhere else. If any real composition seeds a de\n  Bruijn kernel's free environment with a closure, say so and R3\n  becomes a design question instead of a refusal.\n\n---\n","numLines":130,"startLine":2571,"totalLines":2728}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UrfVFXBYF9aAffmeALCzmd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
